# Response to "I Have No Mouth and I Must Scream... is a Joke"

## Video Script — Draft 1

---

### ACT 1: Trying to Understand Ellison

So I watched this video essay about *I Have No Mouth and I Must Scream*. It's long, it's ambitious, it argues that the short story is structurally a joke — setup, hook, development, punchline — and then it builds outward from there into Žižek, Alan Watts, Buddhist numerology, Freudian psychoanalysis, and eventually arrives at something about acceptance and impermanence and learning to play the game of life.

And I respect it. I do. If that's how you read the story, that's a valid reading. But I wasn't convinced. And the more I sat with it, the more I realized my problem wasn't with any single claim — it was with the framing. The essay processes Ellison's rage through a series of philosophical lenses that sound sophisticated but quietly strip the work of the thing that made it dangerous in the first place.

So I did what I always do when something bothers me. I went looking.

---

Not at Ellison directly — I didn't know the man, I can only piece things together from interviews and writing. But at the world that shaped him. Because the essay talks about the Cold War, and it's not wrong, but it treats it as backdrop. Atmosphere. I think it was more specific than that.

Here's what the timeline actually looks like:

1942 — Asimov publishes the Three Laws of Robotics. An optimist's answer to Frankenstein: if we're going to build things smarter than us, let's build in the guardrails. The assumption is benign intent. You're building a helper, a servant, and you're asking how to keep it safe.

1956 — SAGE, the Semi-Automatic Ground Environment, gets introduced to the public. A continent-spanning computer network designed to coordinate America's air defense. It goes fully operational by '63. Not a helper. Not a servant. A war machine.

1958 — Peter George publishes *Red Alert* under the name Peter Bryant. A novel about how terrifyingly easy it would be to start a nuclear war and wipe each other out. Just... procedurally easy. The systems are built for it.

1961 — Kubrick starts working on *Dr. Strangelove*. What begins as a serious adaptation of *Red Alert* becomes a comedy, because Kubrick realizes the material is already absurd. You can't satirize something that's already a joke on itself. Relinquishing free will to automated systems, and it ends in nuclear fire.

1962 — The Cuban Missile Crisis. And now the dread that had been simmering in think tanks and defense labs goes mainstream. By '64, everyone knows. Everyone feels it. The systems are too big, too fast, too difficult to control.

And behind all of this — something that's easy to forget — there was a deliberate awareness campaign. Duck and cover. Bert the Turtle. Civil defense drills in schools. New York City spent what would be almost two million dollars today on identification bracelets for students. Dog tags. For children. With the unspoken understanding that they would help identify bodies after a nuclear explosion.

And if you tried to tell the truth about what a nuclear attack would actually look like — if you were Peter Watkins, who made *The War Game* for the BBC in 1965, a docudrama showing burns, firestorms, radiation sickness, societal collapse — your film got shelved. Kept from the public for twenty years. Censored. Not because it was inaccurate, but because it was too accurate.

So if you were paying attention — if you were Kubrick, if you were Ellison — you saw two things at once: the machines getting bigger, and the lies getting louder. The gap between what was real and what people were allowed to know was widening every year. And for the people who could see that gap, it must have felt like being robbed of your voice.

Then Kubrick releases *Dr. Strangelove*, and as David Greenberg wrote, "America learned to stop worrying and love the bomb. The film not only pointed up the absurdities of the nation's nuclear policy but also showed that laughter constituted a saner reaction than panic."

And I think Ellison agreed with that — partly. Laughter over panic, yes. But I think a gnawing itch was still bothering him. The idea that this scenario could be enacted again. That while it's good to act in a way that enriches us instead of paralyzes us, it's also good to remember that control is a precious thing, and once it's gone, there's no guarantee you'll have the luxury to laugh about it.

1967 — AM is born.

---

Now, the essay I'm responding to talks about Ellison seeing suffering as "the closest thing to a universal law." I don't think that's quite right.

From *Paingod and Other Delusions*, published in 1965 — right around the time he was writing *I Have No Mouth* — Ellison wrote that pain is "the most important thing in the universes. Greater than survival, greater than love, greater even than the beauty it brings about. For without pain, there can be no pleasure. Without sadness, there can be no happiness. Without misery there can be no beauty. And without these, life is endless, hopeless, doomed and damned."

That's not suffering as cosmic law. That's suffering as precondition for meaning. Without it, nothing else registers. Which is a deeply humanistic position, even though it sounds bleak on the surface. Nobody likes to sweat when they exert physical effort, but everyone understands why it's better to do so than not. Suffering is a byproduct of existing, and we have the cognition to see it's not a gratuitous evil.

The point wasn't to out-torture reality. Ellison was angry, not sadistic. He wrote the story in a single night, and by most accounts the driving emotion was rage — at the Vietnam War, at the mechanization of violence, at the way systems dehumanize people. AM is the amalgamation of feelings of hate, panic, and destruction, selectively poured into a machine. An exercise in Murphy's Law. Of course that's what it's made of.

He didn't write to fix suffering. He wasn't offering solutions or programs or three laws that would make it stop. He was grabbing you by the collar and forcing you to look, because he believed that looking away was how you got there.

And this is where Asimov and Ellison diverge completely. Asimov was an engineer at heart. He believed in solvable problems. Ellison was a moralist. He believed the problem wasn't technical — it was us. We were building machines designed to maximize death. How do you integrate sterilized rules into machines born out of hatred? They weren't built for us — they were built for the enemy. But with what parameters does one define the enemy?

Asimov's Laws assume you're building a robot to help. You start from benign intention and add guardrails. Ellison was looking at what was actually being built. Not household robots. SAGE. War computers. Command and control systems whose entire purpose was to calculate how to kill millions of people more efficiently.

We read the story in first person. We're in Ted's mind. And we're angry. The reader is angry. And when the book gets closed, one hopes the reader resolves to not allow this to happen. Ellison feared for the world. He was looking at the rate of things — how fast the weapons were proliferating, how fast the technology was advancing, how fast the political situation was deteriorating — and thinking: this doesn't plateau. This doesn't reach an equilibrium. It just keeps going. And if it keeps going, the suffering doesn't just continue. It compounds.

*I Have No Mouth* is what happens when you project that curve out far enough.

