### ACT 2: Sitting With the Essay

This is where I get into the philosophy. Not because I'm an authority — I'm not — but because the essay invites these thinkers into the room, and once they're in, they're fair game.

---

**Žižek and Jouissance**

The essay leans on Žižek's framing of the id-machine — AM as a device that materializes your unconscious fears and desires. And Žižek has this broader idea, borrowed from Lacan's concept of jouissance, that people become attached to their suffering. That there's a dark compulsion that keeps us returning to what harms us.

I find this pretty lazy, if I'm honest.

Nobody gets attached to their suffering. Not even me, and I prefer it to numbness. There's a difference between understanding that suffering has shaped you and wanting it. I can recognize that my experience of pain gave me something — depth, perspective, whatever — without enjoying it, seeking it out, or being attached to it.

And the broader political version of Žižek's argument has the same problem. When he says people are attached to the systems that oppress them, it slides into blaming them for their own oppression. Oh, they must be getting something out of it on some unconscious level. Which is a very comfortable thing for a philosopher to say from a lectern. It lets the system off the hook. Instead of asking why the system is so effective at trapping people, you're asking why people are so perversely drawn to being trapped.

Ellison would never have made that argument. His whole point was that the people inside AM's hell aren't there because of some deep psychological attachment to suffering. They're there because something more powerful than them put them there and won't let them leave. The horror isn't that they secretly want it. The horror is that they absolutely don't, and it doesn't matter.

Now, jouissance is Lacan's term, not Žižek's. And it doesn't mean what people think it means. It's not enjoyment — it's the point where intensity pushes past pleasure into something that is simultaneously pleasurable and painful. The compulsion to keep going anyway. Lacan was trying to answer why people repeatedly do things that clearly harm them, in ways that can't be explained by simple pleasure-seeking.

But I think the real answer is simpler and *xxxHolic* gets at it better than Lacan does. The person who stays in a bad pattern isn't enjoying suffering as a concept. They're afraid of who they'd be without it. That's not attachment to pain. That's attachment to identity. And those are completely different things. Yūko's whole framework is about the price of wishes — people come to her because they want something to change, but the cost of change is giving up the thing that defines them, even when that thing is destroying them. The suffering has become so entangled with their sense of self that removing it feels like annihilation.

---

**Alan Watts and the Furnace**

The essay quotes Watts: "By going right down to the bottom of the furnace, no further pains will harass you." And frames suffering as something you walk into, accept, and transcend.

Telling someone in the middle of real agony to just stop running and be present with it is like telling someone drowning to just enjoy the water. The advice requires a calm you don't have access to when you actually need it. It only works in retrospect.

And there's a deeper problem with Watts. He wrote that "every willful effort to improve the world or oneself is futile." Which is a comfortable thing to believe if you're a well-fed British man living on a houseboat in Sausalito. And I love that for him — it's genuinely satisfying to see someone live their best life.

But the world isn't static. It degrades. Systems rot. Rights erode. Infrastructure crumbles. What looks like standing still is actually sliding backward, because entropy doesn't wait for your permission. The status quo isn't a resting place — it's a treadmill too. You have to run just to stay where you are.

Watts' position conveniently exempts him from that work. "Every willful effort to improve the world is futile" is a philosophy that only survives in comfort. The moment the water turns brown, you stop contemplating the Tao and you fix the pipe.

Ellison understood this instinctively. His whole life was willful effort directed at a world he thought was deteriorating. He sued people. He screamed. He mailed dead animals to publishers. Because he could see it getting worse, and refusing to act was complicity.

---

**The Daimon**

So if Žižek says we're attached to our suffering, and Watts says stop trying to fix anything — what's the alternative?

I kept coming back to an older concept. The daimon.

Socrates described it like this: "The favor of the gods has given me a marvelous gift, which has never left me since my childhood. It is a voice which, when it makes itself heard, deters me from what I am about to do and never urges me on." Heraclitus said, "Character is daimon for the human." Plato described daimons as intermediaries — "interpreting and transporting human things to the gods and divine things to men."

The daimon doesn't give instructions. It only says don't. Like a mother cat with a cub — it lets you roam, it trusts you can handle things, and it only intervenes when it sees you heading toward real danger.

The superego, by contrast, doesn't trust you at all. It hovers constantly, micromanaging, assuming you'll go wrong the moment it looks away. It treats you like you're already guilty.

And this distinction matters for Ellison. Because Ellison was an atheist, and unapologetic about it. He said religion was presumptuous because it lets people avoid responsibility by blaming an unseen deity. But his work is drenched in religious imagery. AM is essentially a god — omnipotent, omniscient, and honest about its cruelty. *The Deathbird* makes God the villain. *Paingod* is about a cosmic entity whose job is distributing suffering.

I think the gods in Ellison's stories come from the fiction he was raised on. He was Jewish, and Judaism — more than most traditions — is formal, historically charged, and saturated with stories about suffering, endurance, and the question of what you owe the world when the world owes you nothing. But I don't think his insight into suffering came from biblical literature alone. It came from Painesville, Ohio, where a Jewish kid got the shit kicked out of him for being Jewish — in the same country that had just liberated the concentration camps. His experience gave him a specific lens: the awareness of being part of a humanity that could turn on you at any moment, as long as there was a metric they could exclude you by.

Sometimes the daimon says don't harm. But sometimes what it's saying don't to is inaction. Don't sit here. Don't let this slide. Don't pretend stillness is wisdom when the ground is moving under you. Ellison's whole life was daimon-driven, and the daimon was screaming.

---

**The Characters — What the Essay Gets Wrong**

I want to go through these, not as a takedown, but because the essay does something consistent across all five characters that I think distorts them.

**Ted.** The essay says Ted "does not transcend the game, he plays it perfectly" and "emerges morally consistent, narratively intact." But Ted in the book is a narcissistic womanizer who took advantage of people. If he comes out of his trial having genuinely sacrificed for Ellen without expecting anything in return, that's not consistency — that's transformation. Calling it consistency undersells what happened to him. And "remaining internally coherent" for book-Ted means staying a narcissist. The game version of Ted loves Ellen — maybe not more than himself, but in equal parts — and chooses her over himself. That's change, not coherence.

**Gorrister.** The essay says Gorrister "discovers that clarity means letting go of suffering as identity." But Gorrister isn't clinging to suffering as identity. He's drowning in guilt because he thinks he destroyed someone he loved. That's not an identity choice. That's a wound. "Letting go of suffering as identity" makes it sound like he was enjoying being miserable, like if someone told him to stop identifying with his pain he'd be fine. That's therapy-speak flattened into a bumper sticker. What Gorrister actually does is learn that the thing he's guilty about wasn't entirely his fault. That's not letting go of identity. That's receiving new information and adjusting. The most ordinary human thing possible.

**Nimdok.** The essay applies Hannah Arendt's "banality of evil" to Nimdok's complicity. But that's wrong for Nimdok specifically. Eichmann was a paper pusher moving train schedules. Nimdok was a scientist working alongside Mengele, doing experiments on living people. That's not abdication of thought. That's hands in the body. Arendt's point was about thoughtlessness — mundane administrative complicity. Nimdok's evil isn't banal. It's intimate. He was there. He saw. He chose to keep going. "Banality of evil" has become an all-purpose explanation for any Nazi behavior, but Arendt was very specific, and this isn't it.

**Ellen.** The essay says "Ellen's fear is not weakness — it's competence that outlived its context." Sure, but who implied it was weakness? Then it says she "does not conquer her fear" and "reclaims agency at the moment where fear no longer gets to decide for her." And then later: "This is not a triumph over fear." But that IS triumph over fear. What else would triumph over fear look like? Fear doesn't vanish for anyone, ever. Triumph was always "it's there and I act anyway." And "she is not cured" — nobody said anything about cured. Nobody recovers from what happened to Ellen by completing a puzzle in a yellow pyramid. The essay is so careful about being sensitive that it ends up contradicting itself.

**Benny.** The essay says "being in the shit together collapses hierarchy faster than ideology ever could." I'd argue the opposite. Shared hardship doesn't flatten hierarchy — it replaces one with another. Rank dissolves but competence immediately takes its place. The radio guy becomes king. The medic becomes god. You didn't flatten anything; you reshuffled who's on top based on who's useful right now.

What's actually happening with Benny is a liminal experience — Victor Turner's concept from anthropology. When someone is stripped of their social position entirely and enters a threshold state, they can experience what Turner called communitas: a deep, unstructured human connection that's impossible when hierarchy is intact. Benny, once a military commander, is reduced to something barely human. And in that reduction, embedded in vulnerability alongside a mother and child who share his hunger — that's where genuine empathy becomes possible.

And he has AM to thank for it. I hate that. But it's true.

---

**The 108 Connection**

The essay argues that Ellison's choice of 109 years maps onto the 108 kleshas of Buddhist tradition — one year past the full cycle of mental afflictions. It's a clever reading. And I'll give it this: there is something genuinely beautiful about how humans across cultures and centuries keep circling back to the same concepts.

But did Ellison ever mention Buddhism? Kleshas? Samsara? The number 108? No. The essay just asserts it and moves on. There's a difference between "this reminds me of" and "this is what the author intended." The first is honest and interesting. The second needs evidence.

And then the essay says the kleshas — craving, aversion, fear, pride, hatred, ignorance — are "not sins or moral failures but habits of perception." Calling them habits of perception strips the responsibility out. It makes everything a software glitch. Some of those are genuinely perceptual — ignorance, sure. You don't know what you don't know. But pride? Pride is a stance you take. You look at someone and decide you're above them. That's not a perception error. That's a value judgment. Flattening hatred and ignorance into the same bucket sounds wise and falls apart when someone's actually hating you.

---

**Solaris**

One more thing. The essay calls AM an id-machine, "like Solaris but if the ocean hated you personally." But the ocean on Solaris doesn't give you what you want. It gives you what you can't forgive yourself for. It materializes guilt, not desire. That's not the id at all.

And more importantly — Lem was adamant that the ocean resists categorization. He deliberately made it alien, not alien as in "human but different," but alien as in incomprehensible. Everyone keeps trying to make Solaris into something human — a mirror, an unconscious, a god. Lem hated all of it.

The ocean of Solaris floods the valleys that lost loves carved in people, and the people add their own trauma to it. How is that in any way similar to AM? AM is deliberate. AM is personal. AM knows exactly what he's doing. The ocean is indifferent resonance. One is a world with a malevolent author. The other is a world with no author at all.

---

### ACT 3: Free Knowledge

I want to end with the literary references, because even where they don't fit perfectly, they're still worth knowing.

The essay connects Ellen to Charlotte Perkins Gilman's *The Yellow Wallpaper*. And I can see it — the color yellow as trigger, confinement, internalized fear. But in the original story, Ellen is barely a character. She has almost no agency. The game gave her puzzles, an Egyptian tomb, a choice about confronting her trauma. It made her competent. Which actually moves her further from *The Yellow Wallpaper*, not closer. The color yellow alone doesn't make a literary reference.

Ted gets Faust and Dante. Fine — clever men bargaining with damnation. I can see it.

Benny gets *The Lottery*. And this is the biggest stretch. In Shirley Jackson's story, the violence has no reason. That's the whole point. The town stones someone to death every year because they've always done it. The horror is the absence of why. Benny's scenario has a very specific why — AM built it, AM runs it, AM watches. It's like that experiment where monkeys are conditioned to fear something, and then the ones who know the reason are slowly removed until only the habit remains. That's Jackson's Lottery. Benny's world is different. He knows exactly who's doing this to him. And when he offers himself as sacrifice, he's not submitting to a meaningless ritual — he's depriving AM of its tribute. That's defiance. That's satisfying in a payback kind of way. The Lottery offers no such satisfaction.

And sacrifices to silent gods have been made since the beginning of time. At least AM has the decency to tell you he hates you.

For Nimdok, the essay doesn't offer a literary parallel. And I tried to find one — Kafka's *The Trial*, maybe, where guilt is assumed before it's articulated. But Kafka's horror is bureaucratic absurdity — guilt without crime. Nimdok's horror is the opposite. The crime is real. The guilt is earned. Maybe Primo Levi — *If This Is a Man* — the grey zone where victims are forced into complicity. Or Dostoevsky's Grand Inquisitor, who believes cruelty serves a higher purpose. But honestly? Nimdok doesn't need a literary parallel. His evil is historical and documented. You don't need fiction to package the Holocaust. It already exceeds what fiction can invent.

None of these parallels are precise. They're all in the neighborhood — close enough that someone writing a paper could make the case, far enough that someone who actually cares would push back. And that's fine. They're still free knowledge. They're still valuable. They don't have to tie into a perfect bow by distorting the source.

---

**Petrov and Arkhipov**

One last thing. The original essay opens with Stanislav Petrov and Vasili Arkhipov as examples of individuals who saved the world by refusing to trust the machine. And they did — I'm not disputing the facts. But framing them as singular heroes actually undermines the essay's own point.

They weren't uniquely positioned. They were whoever happened to be on shift. The system that almost ended the world didn't care who was sitting at the console. It just needed someone to say yes. Petrov said no. Arkhipov said no. But the horror isn't that heroes saved us. The horror is that it came down to whether the right person happened to be in the chair that day.

That's not a story about individual heroism defeating the machine. That's a story about luck. And Ellison knew that luck runs out.

---

Can you imagine Ellison alive for ChatGPT? The man who sued James Cameron over *Terminator*, who fought AOL in court over people sharing his stories online, who once mailed a dead gopher to a publisher who owed him money — that guy confronting a world where AI companies scraped the entire internet to train models that can generate passable imitations of human writing?

He would have been absolutely incandescent. And the thing is, he would have been right about a lot of it. Not in a Luddite way, but in the specific Ellison way of saying: you built this thing out of all of us without asking, it exists to replace the human voice, and you're telling me this is progress?

He didn't write to fix suffering. He wrote to make you look. And looking away — letting a system, any system, do your thinking for you — was always, for Ellison, how you end up inside the machine.

